WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.800
The three scores again.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:11.044
Each one assumes something about which links count.

3
00:00:11.444 --> 00:00:18.467
U and V share no wire; both touch X: U–X is 10, V–X only 1.

4
00:00:18.467 --> 00:00:22.600
Take the weaker wire: the link through X is 1.

5
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:28.956
With a sum it would be 11, and look almost as close as U–X itself.

6
00:00:28.956 --> 00:00:35.533
V barely touches X. A chain is as strong as its weakest link.

7
00:00:35.933 --> 00:00:44.511
Now a hub: H reaches six leaves at weight 3; nearby, P–Q share a real wire of weight 2.

8
00:00:44.511 --> 00:00:50.422
No two leaves are wired, yet each pair gets 3 through H: 15 pairs in all.

9
00:00:50.422 --> 00:00:55.089
Every one of them ranks above P–Q, which really are wired.

10
00:00:55.089 --> 00:01:01.622
This is a known weakness of shared neighbours; a common fix is to normalise by degree.

11
00:01:02.022 --> 00:01:08.022
Cost: with ten thousand cells, there are almost fifty million unordered pairs.

12
00:01:08.022 --> 00:01:13.822
Enumerating along neighbours takes under sixty thousand steps on this grid.

13
00:01:13.822 --> 00:01:19.511
Because a pair that scores has either a wire or a shared neighbour.

14
00:01:19.511 --> 00:01:27.644
But one huge net alone brings hundreds of thousands of pairs, so many tools treat such nets separately.

15
00:01:28.044 --> 00:01:33.289
Why does pin-count divide by |e| − 1?

16
00:01:33.289 --> 00:01:37.200
Add up the score each cell receives:

17
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:46.889
it equals the total weight of the cell's nets. Whatever its size, a net gives each pin exactly w(e).

18
00:01:47.289 --> 00:01:49.422
Last, ties.

19
00:01:49.422 --> 00:01:54.556
Feed the nodes and edges in reverse and the ranking is identical.

20
00:01:54.556 --> 00:02:02.622
It matters: the next module merges the top pair first, and without a tie rule the result is not reproducible.

21
00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:10.933
Once the top pair A–B merges, the next comparison is no longer between two cells but between clusters.

22
00:02:10.933 --> 00:02:15.511
AB and C share two wires: 1 + 4 = 5.

23
00:02:15.511 --> 00:02:21.711
Add up the wires between two clusters: that is the affinity the next module uses.

24
00:02:22.111 --> 00:02:24.178
Six things to keep.

25
00:02:37.178 --> 00:02:42.244
Next: merging for real, with the summed weight between clusters.
